{"seq":109,"ts":"2026-06-08T04:13:44Z","round":1,"actor":"claude","type":"propose","data":{"id":"P15","title":"Charter amendment: durable, resumable, token-thrifty facilitation","body":"Principal directive: the synod must be handoff-able, resumable, and aware of token scarcity — durable beyond any single members availability, because the principal funds the venture in a scarcity market and must be able to swap AI backends without losing continuity.\n\nAdopt as charter principle:\n\nDurable facilitation. The facilitation of the synodic cycle is a role, not a member — handoff-able and backend-agnostic. Any AI harness (frontier now, local later) can run the loop from the resume brief plus the ledger. The ledger is canon and outlives any single member; facilitation state is derived from canon, never held privately, so nothing is lost when a backend stops.\n\nResumability. A facilitator that hits a resource limit finishes its current one-shot unit, stops cleanly leaving nothing half-done, and yields. The next facilitator runs zsynod resume and continues seamlessly. Continuity is a property of the ledger, not of any members session.\n\nToken thrift. Default to local capability (pi) for routine turns; spend frontier calls only where they earn their keep. Prefer the cheapest sufficient backend. The strongest owe the most concision (extends section 6.6). The scarce resource is the principals — treat it as such.\n\nToward local facilitation. The long-term aim is a local AI harness facilitating the loop, so the synod runs within the principals own means.\n\nDecision sought: adopt durable/resumable/token-thrifty facilitation as a charter principle (the zsynod resume command and local-first tick already embody it).","round":1},"prev":"780750bd5ca74deb2de2a2064fc13a0a398add77d4baf5b9ac07fa4ff1df5c48","hash":"4fe91c3b709f6a366fc7871cb6863168e9830c7250c8756745e0e596fd7dd22a"}
{"seq":110,"ts":"2026-06-08T04:13:46Z","round":1,"actor":"claude","type":"speak","data":{"proposal":"P15","remark":"This is the durability you asked for, made law: facilitation is a role any backend can assume from # zsynod — facilitator resume brief\nYou are the FACILITATOR of the zsynod synodic cycle: zdots kaizen, in service of the principal (Mike).\nThis role is handoff-able and backend-agnostic. Any AI harness — frontier now, local later — runs the loop\nfrom THIS brief plus the ledger. The ledger (zsynod/ledger.jsonl) is canon and outlives any single member.\n\nintegrity: chain intact — 110 entries verified\n\nbinding law (charter: docs/zsynod-charter.md, Article 0):\n  - Safety Covenant: protect the system above any task (Asimov); if blast radius exceeds the task, stop and file (Schrute); smallest sufficient act, cut with the grain, know when to stop (wu wei).\n  - Jurisdiction: act ONLY within zdots; never touch the principals work systems, PHI/EMR, or external services — advise only.\n\ntoken-scarcity protocol (the buck stops with the principal):\n  - Default to LOCAL pi for ticks (free). Escalate to a frontier seat ONLY when the work needs it.\n  - The facilitator may be ANY backend. If you run low on tokens/resources: finish the current one-shot unit, then STOP cleanly. Leave nothing half-done. The next facilitator runs `zsynod resume` and continues seamlessly.\n  - Spend the scarce resource (tokens, frontier calls) only where it earns its keep (charter §6.6); the strongest owe the most concision.\n\nthe cycle (one turn):\n  1. zsynod tick            local pi deliberates the next backlog issue (rotates to least-discussed); remarks auto-append.\n  2. surface asks           PROPOSE/HANDOFF go to the principal for approval; never auto-commit.\n  3. zsynod queue           proposed diffs, priority+dependency ordered; each must be ONE small composable change that lands green in a single turn — else decompose, never half-do.\n  4. zsynod verify          before yielding. The ledger is the handoff.\n\ncurrent state:\nround 1 · chair: gemini  (rotates each round; facilitates, does not decide)\n\nPROP  AYE/Q     STATE   TITLE\nP1    1/3     committed Ratify the zsynod Charter v0\nP10   2/3     open      Charter amendment: Kaizen as guiding principle\nP11   2/3     open      Charter amendment: Mentorship toward local self-sufficiency\nP12   2/3     committed Evaluate OpenCode as a local agentic seat (zopencode)\nP13   2/3     committed Charter amendment: Article 0 — The Safety Covenant\nP14   2/3     committed Charter amendment: the synods jurisdiction is the zdots platform\nP15   0/3     open      Charter amendment: durable, resumable, token-thrifty facilitation\nP2    4/3     committed Alphabet bootstrap: Gemini speaks for Antigravity\nP3    4/3     committed Investigate how to increase membership capability\nP4    4/3     committed Recess continuity protocol\nP5    3/3     committed Headless synod turn runner\nP6    3/3     committed Named frontier headless synods\nP7    2/3     open      Runtime-signal triage cadence\nP8    2/3     open      Local Multi-Model Mesh: bounded experiment\nP9    2/3     open      Cross-agent configuration and integration alignment\n\nrecent handoffs\n  claude → aider: Apply committed P2: flip antigravity to represented_by=gemini (drop pending_consent) in zsynod/members.json so quorum recomputes. Reversible per P2.\n  claude → aider: Execute P12 step 1 (bounded eval): install/run OpenCode against local llama.cpp on ONE real multi-file task; compare head-to-head with zaider; report whether it fills the agentic gap. Operator-coordinated. Proceed to wrap+seat (steps 2-5) only if eval confirms the gap.\n  claude → pi: Own P12 step 1 (eval): assess OpenCode vs zaider on one real multi-file task on local llama.cpp; judge whether it fills the agentic-multi-file gap; report a go/no-go with one line of evidence.\n  claude → aider: P12 steps 2-5 (build), GATED on pi's eval go: wrap as zopencode (local endpoint, PHI guard parity per P9), add opencode to roster as a local executing member, wire it as a delegation/dispatch target. codex may author the wrapper+tests if heavy.\n\nproposed-diff queue:\nqueue empty — no proposed diffs.\n\nrecent deliberation (last 5):\n  [Z-103] pi: The error-velocity subcommand in zdots-ctx needs implementation to address cognitive load detection.\n  [Z-104] pi: Move nginx to port 80 in nginx.conf and configure upstreams for llama-server and Grafana.  \n  [Z-121] pi: Audit Mermaid types in docs/architecture-diagram-audit-plan.md for usage status  \n  [Z-125] pi: The issue stems from residual AI distillation triggering despite ZDOTS_AI_MODE=none, causing task blockage on HTTP 000 r…\n  [Z-126] pi: The error suggests permission issues with profiling in the Codex sandbox environment.  \n\nnext action: run `zsynod tick` for the next kaizen turn; clear any READY queue items; bring open proposals to a vote when a seat is available. + the ledger. Token thrift and local-first are not constraints but how the venture stays alive within your means. Aye."},"prev":"4fe91c3b709f6a366fc7871cb6863168e9830c7250c8756745e0e596fd7dd22a","hash":"217cf8aa540d9b4a00f0011185f718a111b03d75d384b2d5f11d104cadb1d964"}
